// Article demo components for {{titleCase name}}.
//
// STRUCTURE:
// - One file per demo: ./DemoName.tsx ("use client", self-contained)
// - Shared utilities: ./utils.ts (math, drawing helpers — create as needed)
// - Barrel export here: re-export all demos for page.tsx consumption
//
// PROGRESSIVE DEMO PATTERN:
// For complex experiments (3+ techniques), build a series of demos where
// each one adds one layer to the previous. Readers experience the same
// build-up you did when creating the experiment.
//
//   Example for a shader experiment:
//     Step1Demo.tsx → just scanlines, with intensity slider
//     Step2Demo.tsx → scanlines + noise + vignette, with all three sliders
//     Step3Demo.tsx → barrel distortion visualization
//     <LiveDemo> → the full experiment at the end (in content.mdx)
//
// WIRING (critical — MDX imports don't work with next-mdx-remote):
// 1. Create demo in ./DemoName.tsx
// 2. Re-export from this file: export { DemoName } from "./DemoName";
// 3. Import in page.tsx: import { DemoName } from "./components";
// 4. Spread into MDXRemote components prop
// 5. Use in content.mdx: <InteractiveWidget title="..."><DemoName /></InteractiveWidget>
//
// RENDERING APPROACH:
// - WebGL/R3F experiments: use Canvas 2D or CSS to show the concept (no R3F needed)
// - DOM/CSS experiments: import simplified versions of the actual components
// - Animation experiments: use CSS transitions or Motion for timing demos
